{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"agent_message","text":"FAIL\n\n1. **SEVERITY: critical**  \n   **CLAIM:** A `COMPLETED` report can be produced after the implementing agent has reviewed its own work.  \n   **LOCATION:** `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:278-288`, `src/gnosis/kernel/convergence.py:350-375`  \n   **REPRODUCTION:** Configure `TaskEngine.cli_runner` and `GovernedPipeline.review_runner` with the same CLI runner/agent identity—the normal test fixture does exactly this with one `_Agent`. Have implementation succeed and return a PASS review. The pipeline reports `COMPLETED`.  \n   **WHY IT MATTERS:** No code establishes that the reviewer is independent from the implementer; `reviewer_id` is only a label. This breaks rule 10 (“INDEPENDENT BEFORE INTERACTION”) and makes ADR-0017’s claim that completion requires an “independent review” false.\n\n2. **SEVERITY: critical**  \n   **CLAIM:** A crashing fixer strands a consumed brief with no final report, escalation, or terminal/parked record.  \n   **LOCATION:** `src/gnosis/adapters/cli_review.py:260-265`, `src/gnosis/kernel/convergence.py:342-347`, `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:185-231`  \n   **REPRODUCTION:** Return a blocking FAIL review in round 1, then make the runner raise `RuntimeError` only for the fixer prompt. `CliFixer` propagates it; `ConvergenceLoop` intentionally propagates fixer exceptions; `run_brief` catches only `CredentialHeld` and `LaunchRefused` around convergence.  \n   **WHY IT MATTERS:** The record remains `IN_PROGRESS` after it was already claimed/consumed, and no `EngineerReport` or convergence evidence is written. This violates rules 3–4 and the ADR’s “one governed and recorded … run” claim. It also exposes the stated exception-boundary mismatch between reviewer failures (absorbed) and fixer failures (unhandled).\n\n3. **SEVERITY: major**  \n   **CLAIM:** Convergence policy verdicts are not durably recorded.  \n   **LOCATION:** `src/gnosis/runner/gated_runner.py:121-127`, `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:268-294`, `src/gnosis/kernel/convergence.py:173-181`  \n   **REPRODUCTION:** Run a successful brief under an allowing policy, then inspect `<outbox>/<task_id>-convergence/convergence.json`. It contains convergence findings and evidence failures, but neither the ALLOW/WARN decisions nor their action identities. The `GatedAgentRunner.decisions` list is transient and the pipeline supplies no `on_decision` callback.  \n   **WHY IT MATTERS:** An operator cannot later prove which review/fix launches were authorized. ADR-0017 calls this “one governed and recorded” run and says allowed launches are evidence too; the implementation records neither.\n\n4. **SEVERITY: major**  \n   **CLAIM:** A report for a pre-launch park/refusal contains a run ID that resolves to no run.  \n   **LOCATION:** `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:311-332`  \n   **REPRODUCTION:** Place a credential hold before `run_brief`, or deny the initial implementation launch. The report writes `run_id = \"<task_id>-no-run\"` while `run_ids` is empty and no matching `RunStore` record exists.  \n   **WHY IT MATTERS:** The code explicitly invents a reference it says must not be invented. This breaks the state/evidence requirement and contradicts the comment at lines 315–317.\n\n5. **SEVERITY: major**  \n   **CLAIM:** A lost worktree handle makes convergence review and edit the source repository rather than the implemented worktree.  \n   **LOCATION:** `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:248-261`, `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:278-289`  \n   **REPRODUCTION:** Let implementation succeed in a worktree, then make `WorktreeManager.load_handle(task_id)` raise—for example, through a missing/corrupt handle after implementation. `_exec_root` silently returns `repo_path`; verifier, reviewer, fixer, and fingerprinting then operate there. A PASS can produce `COMPLETED` for code never reviewed.  \n   **WHY IT MATTERS:** This directly falsifies ADR-0017’s “Convergence reviews the worktree” claim and can cause reviewer/fixer activity in the shared source tree.\n\n6. **SEVERITY: major**  \n   **CLAIM:** The pipeline executes verifier processes outside both the policy gate and rate-limit hold.  \n   **LOCATION:** `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:278-281`, `src/gnosis/kernel/verification.py:59-65`  \n   **REPRODUCTION:** Supply a `CommandVerifier`; it invokes `subprocess.run` directly through `self.verifier.run(exec_root)`. Also place a hold after a failed review: the next convergence round still starts verification before the gated reviewer observes the hold.  \n   **WHY IT MATTERS:** The requested coverage includes verifier launches, and rule 13 requires deny-by-default for sensitive actions. A verifier is configurable process execution, yet no policy verdict can authorize or deny it and no hold binds it.\n\n7. **SEVERITY: major**  \n   **CLAIM:** Calling the public `run_brief` API twice for one `brief_id` overwrites its durable task/evidence linkage.  \n   **LOCATION:** `src/gnosis/director/pipeline.py:151-154`, `src/gnosis/director/brief_record.py:79-86`  \n   **REPRODUCTION:** Invoke `pipeline.run_brief(brief)` twice directly. Each invocation creates a new `task_id`; `BriefRecordStore.create()` overwrites `<brief_id>.json` without checking for an existing record. The first task’s run IDs/report path become unreachable from the brief record.  \n   **WHY IT MATTERS:** `run_pending` prevents normal inbox duplicates, but the central public method does not. This breaks the record’s stated one-brief-to-one-task invariant and loses provenance between runs.\n\nThe loop itself is bounded by `max_rounds`; I found no separate unbounded-loop defect. The ADR accurately discloses that repeated refusals consume the finite round budget, but the defects above remain outside that disclosed limitation."}}
